# multiplai-kit — BASE CONFIG TEMPLATE
# Copy to .env and fill in your values:  cp .env.example .env
#
# .env holds the full base config: workspace path, default git identity,
# GH token, container settings, and secrets (API keys for skills).
# .env is gitignored. Loaded by claude.sh on every launch.
#
# NOT TO BE CONFUSED WITH env.example (no dot), which is a DIFFERENT template
# for per-profile overrides (env.work, env.personal — git identity only).
# See README.md → "Environment Configuration" for the full picture.
#
# HOW THESE REACH THE CONTAINER
#
#   * Every variable you assign here is forwarded into the container when its
#     value is NON-EMPTY. Add a variable, get it in the container — no launcher
#     edit. Empty or unset means not forwarded at all, so an in-container
#     default still applies instead of being shadowed by an empty value.
#   * A COMMENTED-OUT line assigns nothing, so uncommenting is what forwards it.
#   * Exceptions: a short denylist of variables that only configure the launcher
#     itself, or that hold a host path the container mounts elsewhere —
#     IMAGE_NAME, CONTAINER_REPO/REF, KIT_VENV_VOLUME, MULTIPLAI_NET,
#     GH_TOKEN_KEYCHAIN, MULTIPLAI_MOUNT_GEMINI, MULTIPLAI_HUB_*, WORKSPACE,
#     SSH_BUILD_KEY, GCP_KEY_FILE, CLAUDE_CREDENTIALS_FILE, GEMINI_CONFIG_DIR,
#     IDE_LOCK_DIR.
#     (WORKSPACE and the GCP key still reach the container — as the mounted
#     path, not the host one.)
#   * YOUR SHELL WINS. A variable exported before you launch overrides the value
#     here: `GH_TOKEN=$(mint-a-token) ./claude.sh` uses the minted one. This
#     matches how the in-container loaders read .env (`override=False`).

# === Required ===

# Full ABSOLUTE path to your workspace directory (mounted into container at same path).
# IMPORTANT: Use an absolute path, NOT $HOME — $HOME differs between host and container.
WORKSPACE="/Users/youruser/workspace"

# Git identity
GIT_AUTHOR_NAME="Your Name"
GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL="your@email.com"

# === Deep Research Skill (search API keys) ===
# The deep-research pipeline needs at least one search provider. Tavily is
# the primary; Exa is the secondary (semantic search). Both are free and do
# NOT require a credit card.

# Tavily — 1,000 queries/month free. Sign up: https://tavily.com
# TAVILY_API_KEY="tvly-..."

# Exa — 1,000 requests/month free. Sign up: https://exa.ai
# EXA_API_KEY="..."

# Brave Search — 1,000 queries/month free. Sign up: https://brave.com/search/api/
# BRAVE_API_KEY="BSA..."

# Optional overflow providers (one-time free credits):
# Serper.dev — 50K free credits, $1/1K paid (cheapest overflow). Sign up: https://serper.dev
# SERPER_API_KEY="..."
# You.com — $100 free credit (~20K searches). Sign up: https://api.you.com
# YOU_API_KEY="..."

# === GitHub (optional) ===
# Lets Claude use `gh` CLI inside the container (PRs, issues, CI status).
#
# Prefer a FINE-GRAINED personal access token scoped to the repositories you
# actually want an agent to touch, not a classic `repo`-scoped token. A classic
# token carries every repo your account can reach, including org repos and
# anything private you were added to years ago; a fine-grained one is an
# explicit list. Mint at https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new
# — select only the repos you work on here, and grant Contents: read/write,
# Pull requests: read/write, Issues: read/write (add Workflows only if the agent
# needs to touch CI files). Set an expiry and re-mint; this token lives in a
# container running with permissions bypassed.
#
# Option 1: Set directly
# GH_TOKEN="your-gh-token-here"
#
# Option 2: macOS Keychain. Set GH_TOKEN_KEYCHAIN to the Keychain item holding
# the token — the Keychain is only probed when this names an item (there is no
# implicit default lookup):
# GH_TOKEN_KEYCHAIN="gh-token"
#   security add-generic-password -a "$USER" -s "gh-token" -w "$(gh auth token)" -U
#   Note: `gh auth token` returns your *CLI* token, which is usually broadly
#   scoped. For the fine-grained token above, store it explicitly:
#   security add-generic-password -a "$USER" -s "gh-token" -w "github_pat_..." -U
#   Caveat: Keychain lookups fail from SSH sessions (the login keychain is
#   locked there) — over SSH use GH_TOKEN here, or a GitHub App (option 3).
#
# Option 3 (recommended, macOS + host bridge): a GITHUB APP.
# GH_TOKEN_APP="myapp"
#   Names a host-side App profile in ~/.local/state/multiplai-gh-token/<app>/.
#   The App's private key NEVER enters the container: a SessionStart hook mints a
#   fresh ~1-hour installation token over the SSH bridge and stores it in gh's own
#   credential store, and a PreToolUse hook renews it when it runs out — so `gh`
#   and `git` just work, with nothing to type and no long-lived token anywhere.
#   Host setup (creating the App, installing the key): container/docs/gh-app-token.md.
#
#   ONE IDENTITY PER FILE. Declaring GH_TOKEN_APP here alongside GH_TOKEN or
#   GH_TOKEN_KEYCHAIN is a hard launch error, not a precedence rule — they select
#   different GitHub identities. If you use both an App and a PAT, put each in its
#   own env.<profile> and leave both out of .env. See docs/PROFILES.md.

# === Messaging (optional — multiplai-messaging plugin) ===
#
# These tokens can send mail and post to your team's Slack, and every session
# that receives them can use them — including sessions doing unrelated work.
# Setting one here is the decision to hand it over: leave the line commented out
# unless you use the skill. There is no launcher-side gate, and a gate would be
# theatre — .env sits on the bind-mounted kit root, so a session that can read
# files can read the token whether or not it was forwarded as an env var.
#
# Slack: a single user token (xoxp-…). See the slack skill's references/setup.md.
# SLACK_TOKEN="xoxp-..."
#
# Gmail: OAuth is a trio (not one bearer string) — mint once on the Mac host with
# the gmail skill's get_token.py, which prints these three lines to paste here.
# GMAIL_CLIENT_ID="...apps.googleusercontent.com"
# GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET="..."
# GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN="1//..."

# === Container settings (optional, ignored in --local mode) ===

# Docker image name
# IMAGE_NAME="claude-multiplai:local"

# Container tooling source — setup.sh fetches container/ from this repo at this
# ref. Override to track a fork or a different version (defaults shown).
# CONTAINER_REPO="https://github.com/spikelab/multiplai-container"
# CONTAINER_REF="v0.2"

# SSH key for build bridge to macOS host (e.g., Swift/Xcode builds)
# SSH_BUILD_KEY="$HOME/.ssh/container_build_key"

# Username for SSH build bridge — your macOS username on the host.
# Required by the skills that shell out to the host (transcribe,
# youtube-transcript, screen-demo, swift-build).
# SSH_BUILD_USER="$USER"

# LLM proxy URL (leave commented out for direct Anthropic API). Uncomment to
# forward it on every launch; it is also on the launcher's keep-list, so
# exporting it for a single launch (ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=... ./claude.sh) works
# with no .env line at all.
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL="http://host.docker.internal:4000"

# === Google Cloud (optional) ===
#
# Path to a service-account JSON key on the host. Set it and the launcher mounts
# it read-only at /home/agent/.gcp/key.json and points both
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS and CLOUDSDK_AUTH_CREDENTIAL_FILE_OVERRIDE
# there. Unset means no GCP wiring at all; set-but-missing is a hard error rather
# than a silent launch that fails later inside some gcloud call. Absolute path;
# a leading ~ is expanded, and $HOME works (expanded when this file is sourced).
# Also settable per launch (`GCP_KEY_FILE=... ./claude.sh`) or per profile.
# GCP_KEY_FILE="$HOME/.gcp/my-service-account.json"
#
# Default project for gcloud/gsutil inside the container.
# CLOUDSDK_CORE_PROJECT="my-gcp-project"

# Claude credentials file for container auth. Use an absolute path; $HOME is OK
# (expanded when this file is sourced), but not ~ (not expanded inside quotes).
# Override per profile in env.<name> for multi-account setups.
# CLAUDE_CREDENTIALS_FILE="$HOME/.claude-container/credentials.json"

# Gemini CLI credentials — NOT mounted unless you opt in. The image ships no
# gemini binary, so the mount is off by default rather than exposing the host's
# OAuth refresh tokens and prompt history to every session for nothing. Set
# this to 1 if you installed the CLI yourself and want its auth to persist
# across containers. It mounts read-write on purpose: the CLI rewrites its
# credentials file on every token refresh.
# MULTIPLAI_MOUNT_GEMINI=1
#
# Which directory to mount when the above is enabled. Absolute path; $HOME is
# OK (expanded at source time), not ~. Override per profile in env.<name> to
# use different Google accounts per profile.
# GEMINI_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.gemini"

# --- `/ide` from inside the container -----------------------------------------
#
# Connects the containerised CLI to VS Code / Cursor running on the host, so
# `/ide` works and diffs open in the editor instead of scrolling past in chat.
# Two independent pieces; you need BOTH.
#
# 1. The lockfiles. The editor extension writes one per window to ~/.claude/ide/
#    with the port and auth token of its WebSocket server. The launcher mounts
#    that directory read-only at /home/agent/.claude/ide when it exists — no
#    setting required, and nothing happens if you have no extension installed.
#    Set this only to mount a directory from somewhere else.
# IDE_LOCK_DIR="$HOME/.claude/ide"
#
# 2. The host to dial. The CLI defaults to 127.0.0.1, which inside a container
#    is the container — never the editor. Point it at the host:
# CLAUDE_CODE_IDE_HOST_OVERRIDE="host.docker.internal"
#
# REQUIRES ORBSTACK. The extension binds the host's 127.0.0.1 only, and
# OrbStack routes host.docker.internal to host-loopback services. Rootless
# Docker and Docker Desktop sandboxes block exactly this, so on those runtimes
# the override resolves but nothing answers.

# Container egress profile: unrestricted (default) | restricted. Also settable
# per launch: `MULTIPLAI_NET=restricted ./claude.sh` (your shell wins over this
# file). "restricted" is reserved — the launcher refuses it loudly until the
# egress allowlist ships, rather than pretending to confine the container.
# MULTIPLAI_NET=unrestricted

# Docker named volume holding the container's Linux venv. Defaults to a
# per-runtime name derived from this kit checkout's path (basename + path
# checksum), so parallel runtimes never share a venv volume. Set explicitly
# only to pin a specific volume — e.g. KIT_VENV_VOLUME=kit-venv to keep the
# pre-rename volume of an existing install.
# KIT_VENV_VOLUME=

# Multiplai hub (multiplai-gui) — only if you run one. Used by the launcher's
# adoption take-back flow (release-then-resume). URL can also live in
# multiplai.conf; the TOKEN belongs here (secrets stay out of committed files).
# MULTIPLAI_HUB_URL="http://127.0.0.1:8787"
# MULTIPLAI_HUB_TOKEN=""
